<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sumerian King List</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sumerian King List is an ancient Mesopotamian text listing the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and the cities that held kingship. It mixes legendary and historical rulers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a> profiles refer to it as a source.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list.jpg" alt="Sumerian King List" width="320" height="339" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph: unknown&gt; Transcription: Stephen Herbert Langdon (1876-1937) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weld-Blundell_Prism_with_transcription_by_Stephen_Herbert_Langdon_(1876-1937).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The political weakness was the endemic dynastic instability of the Akkadian state: Rimush and Manishtushu are reported to have been murdered by courtiers, and after Shar-Kali-Sharri's death (c. 2193 BCE) the <b>Sumerian King List</b> records four kings in rapid succession with the line &quot;Who was king?</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the earliest records Elam appears as a target of Sumerian raids: the <b>Sumerian King List</b> credits King Enmebaragesi of Kish with a victory over the Elamites, traditionally dated to around 2700 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasties of Lagash are missing from the <b>Sumerian King List</b>; the one tablet that lists its rulers, The Rulers of Lagash, is generally read as a satirical parody of the King List, its thirty kings given improbable reigns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The city’s ancient name had long been known from the <b>Sumerian King List</b>, which counts a dynasty of Mari among the holders of kingship, but its site was found only in 1933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mesannepada is the first of its kings named in the <b>Sumerian King List</b>, and a cylinder inscribed with his name was found in the Royal Cemetery; five more kings followed before the List says the kingship was taken by Awan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Early Dynastic period Uruk’s rulers used the title lugal, king, and the <b>Sumerian King List</b> names a First Dynasty of Uruk including Meskiaggasher, Enmerkar, Lugalbanda and Gilgamesh, who may have lived around 2700 BCE, though none of these early kings is attested in contemporary inscriptions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
